l, and checked metrics for user-service, which didn\'t return any results. Then they checked the pods and found that user-service is running. But since the metrics query didn\'t return anything, maybe the service isn\'t exposing metrics properly, or there\'s an issue with the metrics collection.\n\nThe next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod because if the metrics aren\'t coming through, it could be an application issue. The user tried to get the logs but maybe didn\'t specify the correct pod name. Wait, in the previous step, they used kubectl get pods and found the user-service pod name is user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k. So they need to get the logs for that specific pod. \n\nThe tool exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with the command "kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network" to retrieve the logs. This will help identify any runtime errors, crashes, or issues that might be causing the service to not expose metrics. If there are errors in the logs, that could point to the root cause, like a configuration mistake, dependency issues, or application bugs. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t configured to expose metrics, so checking the deployment or service YAML files could help. But since the user is focusing on logs first, that\'s a good next step. If the logs don\'t show anything, then maybe checking the service\'s configuration or network policies would be necessary. But given the previous steps, logs are the immediate next action.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network"  \nThe `user-service` pod is listed as `Running`, but the earlier `get_metrics` query for `up{job=\\"user-service\\"}` returned no results, indicating potential issues with metrics exposure or service availability. Inspecting the pod logs directly will reveal runtime errors, crashes, or configuration issues causing the service to fail silently. This is critical to identify application-specific faults.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 475, 'prompt_tokens': 3570, 'total_tokens': 4045, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-3c21-7fe0-815e-9430d3c3d328-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3570, 'output_tokens': 475, 'total_tokens': 4045}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9fd683af-dccd-4c2a-9eb6-583c1cdd80df'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which is made up of many microservices. They've already checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But the metrics for the user-service aren't showing up, which is a red flag.\n\nSo, the next step is to look into the logs of the user-service pod. The previous attempt to get metrics with PromQL didn't return any results, which could mean the service isn't exposing metrics correctly, or there's a problem with the metrics server. But since the pod is running, the issue might be application-specific. \n\nBy checking the logs, they can find out if there are any errors or exceptions that the service is throwing. Maybe the service is crashing but getting restarted by the deployment, so the pod stays in a running state but the service isn't functioning properly. The logs would show if there are any runtime errors, misconfigurations, or issues with dependencies like databases or other services.\n\nThe user needs to use the kubectl logs command to get the logs for the user-service pod. The pod name is user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k, which was obtained from the previous kubectl get pods command. This will allow them to see the actual output from the application, which is crucial for identifying the root cause of the problem. \n\nIf the logs show errors, they can then investigate further, maybe check the configuration files, environment variables, or network connectivity. If there's a missing dependency or a misconfigured service, that's where the issue lies. The logs are the first line of defense in diagnosing such issues in a microservices architecture.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bdcf3dfe63ab46ce', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 396, 'prompt_tokens': 4583, 'total_tokens': 4979, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-5279-7da2-a143-9f072a07293c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bdcf3dfe63ab46ce', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4583, 'output_tokens': 396, 'total_tokens': 4979}), ToolMessage(content='[2026-Mar-17 22:10:41.451188] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = a6:0e:ff:c3:a5:a8\n[2026-Mar-17 22:10:41.451469] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = ba6\n[2026-Mar-17 22:10:41.458412] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\n', id='b8785ec3-a8de-468e-8222-ed158476c1ec', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bdcf3dfe63ab46ce'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='14d19f52-488f-4d1d-b7aa-175df525a91c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. They looked at the user-service logs, but the logs don\'t show any obvious errors. The metrics query for user-service didn\'t return any results, which is concerning.\n\nHmm, maybe the user-service isn\'t exposing metrics properly. But why? The logs don\'t mention any problems. Maybe the issue isn\'t with the user-service itself but something else. Since the application uses Thrift RPCs for communication between services, maybe there\'s a problem with the dependencies. \n\nThe user mentioned using Jaeger for traces. If there are issues with service communication, checking the dependency graph could show if there are any failed connections or latency issues. Also, looking at the traces for the user-service might reveal if there are any errors in the RPC calls. \n\nWait, the user already tried get_metrics for user-service and got no results. Maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t configured correctly. But without seeing the metrics, it\'s hard to tell. Alternatively, maybe the service is up but not handling requests properly. \n\nAnother angle: the user-service might be dependent on other services like user-mongodb or user-memcached. If those services are down or have issues, it could cause the user-service to fail. But the deployments for those services are all running. \n\nWait, the user checked the deployments and pods, and they\'re all running. So maybe the problem is with the network or the way services are communicating. Using get_dependency_graph could show if there are any missing or failing dependencies. Also, checking the traces for the user-service might show if there are any errors in the Thrift RPC calls. \n\nSo the next step should be to get the dependency graph to see if there are any issues with the services the user-service depends on. If the dependency graph shows that the user-service isn\'t connecting to necessary services, that could be the root cause. Alternatively, checking the traces for the user-service might reveal specific errors in the RPC calls. \n\nI think the best tool here is get_dependency_graph. It will show the service dependencies and any potential issues in the communication between services. That could help identify if there\'s a problem with the user-service\'s dependencies or the network configuration.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \n"get_dependency_graph"  \nTo investigate potential communication issues between microservices, the dependency graph will reveal relationships and any anomalies in service interactions. Since the `user-service` metrics are missing, this could indicate network issues, misconfigured dependencies, or failed RPC calls. The graph will help identify if dependencies are unreachable or experiencing high latency.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 558, 'prompt_tokens': 3928, 'total_tokens': 4486, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-6848-7a52-874c-a6227e838abf-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3928, 'output_tokens': 558, 'total_tokens': 4486}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='eb9daa46-ec8c-401d-b789-2aac3fc9acb0'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the deployments and pods, found them all running, but the user-service isn't exposing metrics. They looked at the logs and saw the service starting up, but no errors. Now, they want to check the dependency graph to see if there are any issues with the services the user-service depends on or vice versa.\n\nThe get_dependency_graph tool is the next logical step. By getting the dependency graph from Jaeger, they can visualize how the user-service interacts with other services. If there's a problem, like a service not responding or high latency, the graph might show that. Since the user-service isn't reporting metrics, maybe it's not communicating properly with another service, or there's a network issue. The dependency graph can highlight those connections and any anomalies. So, calling get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame, like the last 15 minutes, should provide insights into the service interactions and help pinpoint the root cause.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 15}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9452ee377bcfebed', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 247, 'prompt_tokens': 4904, 'total_tokens': 5151, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-8254-7072-b590-be33bb53cfc5-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 15}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9452ee377bcfebed', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4904, 'output_tokens': 247, 'total_tokens': 5151}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='7ba85fec-ed53-4dff-af5e-711c2897daca', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9452ee377bcfebed'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5d2f51ee-020c-4a62-a3fa-1ab0df0ed6b4'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the services using get_services, looked at deployments with kubectl, checked logs for user-service, and tried to get a dependency graph. The get_dependency_graph returned an empty data set, which might mean there\'s no data or an issue with the tool.\n\nHmm, the user-service\'s metrics weren\'t showing up either. The logs for user-service didn\'t show any errors, just startup info. The dependency graph is empty, so maybe there\'s no traffic or the Jaeger isn\'t capturing it. But since the user-service is part of a social network with Thrift RPCs, maybe the problem is with the service\'s configuration or network policies.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that some microservices might have faults. Since the dependency graph is empty, maybe the user-service isn\'t communicating with others, or the traces aren\'t being collected. Alternatively, maybe the service isn\'t exposed correctly, leading to no traffic. But the deployments are all running. \n\nAnother angle: the user-service might be missing some required dependencies. For example, if it relies on a database or another service that\'s down, but the logs don\'t show that. Or maybe there\'s a misconfiguration in the service\'s environment variables or ports. \n\nSince the get_dependency_graph didn\'t return any data, maybe the next step is to check the service\'s configuration. Using kubectl to describe the user-service deployment or service could show if the ports are correctly exposed or if there are any annotations related to networking. Also, checking the service\'s YAML to see if it\'s set up to handle Thrift RPCs properly. \n\nAlternatively, checking the network policies to ensure that the user-service can communicate with other services. If there\'s a NetworkPolicy blocking traffic, that could cause the dependency graph to be empty. \n\nBut the user has already checked the deployments and pods, which are all running. So maybe the issue is with the metrics not being collected. The get_metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned nothing, which could mean the service isn\'t exposing metrics, or the Prometheus isn\'t scraping them. Checking the service\'s metrics configuration might be necessary. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the service\'s endpoints. If the user-service isn\'t exposing the correct endpoints, then other services can\'t reach it, leading to no traces. Using kubectl to get the endpoints for the user-service could help. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service is crashing but the pod is restarting, but the logs don\'t show that. The pod status is Running, so maybe it\'s a liveness probe issue. Checking the liveness and readiness probes in the deployment could be important. \n\nBut since the logs don\'t show any errors, maybe the problem is with the service\'s dependencies. For example, if the user-service relies on a database that\'s not reachable, but the logs don\'t mention that. \n\nGiven that the dependency graph is empty, the next step should be to check the service\'s network configuration and ensure that it\'s properly exposed and can communicate with other services. Using kubectl to describe the service and deployment would provide more details on the network settings and any potential misconfigurations.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network"  \nTo investigate the network configuration of the `user-service`, checking its service definition will reveal whether it is exposed correctly (e.g., ClusterIP, NodePort, or LoadBalancer), has proper port mappings, and is reachable by other microservices. This is critical to identify network misconfigurations or exposure issues that could prevent communication.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 760, 'prompt_tokens': 4127, 'total_tokens': 4887, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-8f50-7a71-b97a-34730f31e92e-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4127, 'output_tokens': 760, 'total_tokens': 4887}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='99e5158b-af44-402e-8803-2615c2a4903d'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues with the Social Network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the deployments, pods, and logs for the user-service, but the metrics query didn't return any results. The dependency graph also came back empty. Now, they need to check the service configuration for user-service to see if there's a network issue.\n\nThe next step is to use the `kubectl describe service` command to get details about the user-service's Kubernetes service. This will show information like the service type (ClusterIP, NodePort, etc.), ports, selectors, and endpoints. If the service isn't properly configured, it might not be reaching the pods, leading to the metrics not being collected and potential communication issues between services.\n\nBy examining the service description, they can verify if the service is correctly pointing to the user-service pods. If the service is misconfigured, like having the wrong port or not selecting the correct pods, that could explain why the metrics aren't showing up and why there might be issues with the service's availability. This step should help identify any network or configuration problems at the Kubernetes service level.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a8a2f2473f2cdd4c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 275, 'prompt_tokens': 5117, 'total_tokens': 5392, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-b2c7-78c1-b68d-58fe74476f4e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a8a2f2473f2cdd4c', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5117, 'output_tokens': 275, 'total_tokens': 5392}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                     user-service\nNamespace:                social-network\nLabels:                   app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=Helm\nAnnotations:              meta.helm.sh/release-name: social-network\n                          meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: social-network\nSelector:                 service=user-service\nType:                     ClusterIP\nIP Family Policy:         SingleStack\nIP Families:              IPv4\nIP:                       10.101.23.103\nIPs:                      10.101.23.103\nPort:                     9090  9090/TCP\nTargetPort:               9090/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nSession Affinity:         None\nInternal Traffic Policy:  Cluster\nEvents:                   <none>\n', id='96243e12-bdf8-4419-a71e-69cb2c5b2703', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a8a2f2473f2cdd4c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5d2f0825-5f83-476d-bc7a-59c6fa0181c9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service\'s endpoints are listed as <none>, which means there are no pods actually being exposed through this service. Even though the deployment shows the pod is running, the service isn\'t pointing to any endpoints. That\'s a problem because other services can\'t reach the user-service if it\'s not properly linked.\n\nSo, why would the endpoints be empty? Maybe the service\'s selector isn\'t matching any pods. The selector is set to "service=user-service", but the pods might have a different label. For example, if the pods are labeled with "app=user-service" instead of "service=user-service", the service won\'t find them. That would result in no endpoints and the service being unreachable.\n\nI should check the labels on the user-service pods to confirm. Using kubectl describe pod on one of the user-service pods would show the labels. If the selector in the service doesn\'t match the pod labels, that\'s the root cause. Also, maybe the deployment isn\'t correctly configured to label the pods. Alternatively, there could be a typo in the service\'s selector. Once I verify the labels, I can determine if the service is misconfigured and fix it by updating the selector to match the pod labels.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network"  \nTo verify the pod\'s labels and ensure the `user-service` service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels. The empty endpoints in the service suggest a mismatch between the service\'s selector and the pod\'s labels, preventing proper exposure and communication. This is the likely root cause of the service being unreachable.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=350, prompt_tokens=4474, total_tokens=4824, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-c325-7fe3-9e08-de6ab6852648-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4474, 'output_tokens': 350, 'total_tokens': 4824}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f180b565-9fd7-42a6-a998-fe5c92396e82')]]
