ng   0          94s\n', id='719e33e4-42ae-4019-b3ac-75d51b5a254d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bfe0b28bb34647df'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e1fde5af-55ad-42b6-8c15-998aeef807ee'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a social network application with many microservices. So far, they checked the services using get_services, looked at deployments with kubectl, and checked metrics for user-service, which didn\'t return any results. Then they checked the pods and found that user-service is running. But since the metrics query didn\'t return anything, maybe the service isn\'t exposing metrics properly, or there\'s an issue with the metrics collection.\n\nThe next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod because if the metrics aren\'t coming through, it could be an application issue. The user tried to get the logs but maybe didn\'t specify the correct pod name. Wait, in the previous step, they used kubectl get pods and found the user-service pod name is user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k. So they need to get the logs for that specific pod. \n\nThe tool exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with the command "kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network" to retrieve the logs. This will help identify any runtime errors, crashes, or issues that might be causing the service to not expose metrics. If there are errors in the logs, that could point to the root cause, like a configuration mistake, dependency issues, or application bugs. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t configured to expose metrics, so checking the deployment or service YAML files could help. But since the user is focusing on logs first, that\'s a good next step. If the logs don\'t show anything, then maybe checking the service\'s configuration or network policies would be necessary. But given the previous steps, logs are the immediate next action.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-d4b649c56-fs44k -n social-network"  \nThe `user-service` pod is listed as `Running`, but the earlier `get_metrics` query for `up{job=\\"user-service\\"}` returned no results, indicating potential issues with metrics exposure or service availability. Inspecting the pod logs directly will reveal runtime errors, crashes, or configuration issues causing the service to fail silently. This is critical to identify application-specific faults.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 475, 'prompt_tokens': 3570, 'total_tokens': 4045, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfddb-3c21-7fe0-815e-9430d3c3d328-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3570, 'output_tokens': 475, 'total_tokens': 4045}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9fd683af-dccd-4c2a-9eb6-583c1cdd80df'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which is made up of many microservices. They've already checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But the metrics for the user-service aren't showing up, which is a red flag.\n\nSo, the next step is to look into the logs of the user-service pod. The previous attempt to get metrics with PromQL didn't return any results, which could mean the service isn't exposing metrics correctly, or there's a problem with the metrics server. But since the pod is running, the issue might be application-specific. \n\nBy checking the logs, they can find out if there are any errors or